# East Asia batch (1443-1889): every brief rewritten to rules zero to four.
OVERRIDE = True
A = {
"as14430": dict(
 bluf="Korean King Sejong wanted a script that ordinary Koreans could learn, since classical Chinese took years to master and fitted spoken Korean badly. His commission produced Hangul, completed in 1443 and proclaimed in 1446, with letters shaped on the positions of the mouth and tongue. The Confucian official class, whose standing rested on Chinese, kept it out of government for 450 years; it became the script of Korean national identity and near-universal literacy.",
 now="A script learnable in days broke the official class's monopoly on writing and gave Korea a literate public and a national identity separate from China; both Koreas write in it today.",
 story="Korea's written language was classical Chinese, which took years to master and matched spoken Korean poorly. Korean King Sejong and a group of court officials designed Hangul, completed in 1443 and proclaimed in 1446 as the Hunminjeongeum, 'the correct sounds for instructing the people'. Its letter shapes follow the position of the mouth and tongue in forming each sound.",
 why="Korean King Sejong wrote that ordinary people could not express their thoughts in writing because Chinese characters were too hard to learn. A script that fitted Korean speech could spread literacy beyond the aristocracy.",
 then="Confucian officials, whose status rested on mastering Chinese, opposed the script, and it survived among women and commoners and in popular fiction and verse. It became the official script in 1894. Japanese colonial rule from 1910 to 1945 suppressed Korean, which turned Hangul into a symbol of national resistance.",
 fact="Both Koreas keep a national holiday for the alphabet, the only script in the world with one."),
"as14491": dict(
 bluf="The Ming Zhengtong Emperor marched in person against the Oirat Mongols in 1449 to punish a raid and show the dynasty's strength on the steppe. The Oirats destroyed his army at Tumu and took him prisoner. Ming confidence in offensive war on the steppe ended; China turned to a defensive strategy of walls and fortified garrisons that fixed its northern frontier for two centuries.",
 now="The defeat turned the Ming from steppe campaigns to walls: most of the Great Wall standing today was built in brick and stone after Tumu, and the defensive posture it marks held until the dynasty fell in 1644.",
 story="The Ming had restricted the Oirat tribute trade, and Oirat leader Esen raided the frontier in 1449. The young Ming Zhengtong Emperor, urged on by court eunuch Wang Zhen, led a large and poorly supplied army north in person. At Tumu, north-west of Beijing, the Mongols surrounded the exhausted army, killed much of it and captured the emperor.",
 why="Steppe peoples wanted regular access to Chinese markets, and raids followed when the Ming restricted the tribute trade. At court, eunuch Wang Zhen pushed the inexperienced emperor to lead the army himself.",
 trig="An Oirat invasion in 1449, after a quarrel over the size of their tribute-trade missions.",
 then="The court enthroned the emperor's brother as the Jingtai Emperor, and Ming minister Yu Qian organised the defence of Beijing. The Oirats released their prisoner in 1450, and he retook the throne in a palace coup in 1457. The Ming gave up campaigns into Mongolia and in the 1500s rebuilt the Great Wall in brick and stone.",
 fact="Most of the Great Wall seen today is Ming work, built in the century after Tumu."),
"as14673": dict(
 bluf="Rival factions at the court of Japanese shogun Ashikaga Yoshimasa fought to place their own candidate in the succession. Ten years of fighting burned much of Kyoto and settled nothing; the shogunate came out with no real authority over the provinces. Power passed to regional lords, the daimyo, whose century of competition ended in unification under the Tokugawa in 1603.",
 now="The war began Japan's century of 'warring states', in which regional lords built the domains and warrior culture the Tokugawa later locked in place; the rigid central control imposed after 1603 was designed to prevent another collapse of this kind.",
 story="Japanese shogun Ashikaga Yoshimasa named his brother heir in 1464; a son was born the next year and Yoshimasa's wife backed the child. In 1467 the dispute drew in Japan's great lords, led by Hosokawa Katsumoto and Yamana Sōzen. Fighting raged in the capital for ten years and burned much of Kyoto. When it ended in 1477 the Ashikaga shoguns had lost real control of the country.",
 why="The Ashikaga shoguns depended on provincial governors they could not control. When the shogunal succession fell into dispute, the Hosokawa and Yamana factions backed rival candidates.",
 trig="Fighting broke out in Kyoto in 1467 over the shogunal succession and a feud within the Hatakeyama family.",
 then="Central authority collapsed. Lords built domains with their own laws and castles, adopted firearms after 1543 and fought one another until Oda Nobunaga, Toyotomi Hideyoshi and Tokugawa Ieyasu reunified the country between 1568 and 1603.",
 fact="Shogun Ashikaga Yoshimasa left the war to his generals and retired to build the Silver Pavilion and refine the tea ceremony; the war takes its name from the Ōnin era in which it began."),
"as154310": dict(
 bluf="Portuguese traders aboard a Chinese ship driven onto Tanegashima in 1543 sold the island's lord two matchlock muskets. Japanese smiths copied them within a year, and within decades massed musketeers decided battles between the warring lords. The lords who could afford guns in quantity unified the country, and the speed of the copying shows how fast a society under competitive pressure absorbs foreign technology.",
 now="Japan's rapid adoption of guns in the 1500s, and of Western industry after 1868, contradicts the stereotype of a closed society resistant to change; both were selective borrowing under military pressure.",
 story="A storm drove a Chinese ship with Portuguese traders aboard onto Tanegashima island, south of Kyushu, in 1543. Island lord Tanegashima Tokitaka bought two matchlocks and set his smiths to copy them. Japanese smiths had reverse-engineered the muskets within a year.",
 why="Japan's warring lords had every incentive to adopt a military advantage, and skilled swordsmiths could copy the guns.",
 then="Gun production spread across Japan, and warlord Oda Nobunaga used massed volleys at Nagashino in 1575. Portuguese traders and Jesuit missionaries followed the guns. After unification the Tokugawa restricted both guns and foreign contact.",
 fact="'Tanegashima' became the Japanese word for a matchlock."),
"as159013": dict(
 bluf="Japanese regent Toyotomi Hideyoshi set out to finish the unification Oda Nobunaga had begun and to make it permanent. By 1590 he had defeated or won over every rival lord, and he then disarmed the peasants and froze the social classes. The order he fixed held for 270 years under the Tokugawa; his two invasions of Korea failed, devastated the peninsula and left lasting bitterness there.",
 now="Hideyoshi's sword hunt, land survey and separation of samurai from peasants fixed Japan's social order for 270 years; his invasions of Korea poisoned relations with it for four centuries and ended Japanese expansion until 1894.",
 story="Warlord Oda Nobunaga was assassinated in 1582 with Japan half unified. Toyotomi Hideyoshi, who had begun as a foot soldier in Nobunaga's army, took over his armies and alliances. By 1590, with the fall of the Hōjō at Odawara, he had defeated or won over every rival lord. He then disarmed the peasantry and froze the social classes.",
 why="Toyotomi Hideyoshi took over Oda Nobunaga's armies and alliances after Nobunaga's assassination in 1582. He combined force with deals that let defeated lords keep their domains in return for loyalty.",
 then="The 'sword hunt' of 1588 disarmed the peasantry, edicts fixed social status and a national land survey standardised taxes. He invaded Korea in 1592 and 1597 and failed both times. After his death in 1598 Tokugawa Ieyasu won the struggle for power.",
 fact="Korean Admiral Yi Sun-sin, who destroyed Hideyoshi's fleets, is Korea's national hero."),
"as159214": dict(
 bluf="Japanese regent Toyotomi Hideyoshi invaded Korea in 1592 to reach China and replace the Ming at the centre of the East Asian order. His armies took Seoul within weeks; Korean Admiral Yi Sun-sin's fleet cut their supply lines, a Ming army drove them back, and the invasions ended in withdrawal in 1598. Korea was laid waste, the Ming treasury was drained at a critical moment, and Korea kept a lasting memory of Japanese aggression.",
 now="Korea's memory of the invasions and of Admiral Yi still colours its relations with Japan; the war's cost weakened the Ming before their collapse in 1644, and Japan's withdrawal left the Chinese-centred order in place for another 250 years.",
 story="Japanese regent Toyotomi Hideyoshi landed armies in Korea in 1592 after Korea refused passage for an attack on China. The armies took Seoul quickly and pushed north. Korean Admiral Yi Sun-sin's armoured 'turtle ships' cut Japanese supply lines along the coast, and Ming China sent an army. A second invasion in 1597 also failed.",
 why="Having unified Japan, Toyotomi Hideyoshi needed new conquests to reward his lords and armies, and he aimed to replace China at the centre of the East Asian order.",
 trig="Korea refused to let Japanese armies pass through on their way to attack China.",
 then="Korea was ravaged: cities burned, many thousands died, and potters and artisans were carried off to Japan. The cost of intervention helped empty the Ming treasury, one factor in the dynasty's collapse in 1644. Japan withdrew after Hideyoshi's death in 1598, and the Tokugawa later restored relations.",
 fact="Admiral Yi's armoured 'turtle ships' won battles against heavy odds; at Myeongnyang in 1597 he beat a fleet of more than 100 ships with 13. He lost no naval battle in his career."),
"as160015": dict(
 bluf="Tokugawa Ieyasu, the strongest of the regents left by Toyotomi Hideyoshi, fought a coalition loyal to Hideyoshi's child heir for control of unified Japan. He won in a day when key western lords changed sides. The victory gave his house 250 years of rule, and the line between his allies and his defeated enemies shaped Japanese politics until the losers' heirs overthrew the shogunate in 1868.",
 now="The long Tokugawa peace built on this victory created a unified market, high literacy and a trained bureaucracy, which made Japan's rapid modernisation after 1868 possible.",
 story="Toyotomi Hideyoshi's death in 1598 left a child heir and a council of regents who fought for power. On 21 October 1600 about 160,000 samurai met in a foggy valley at Sekigahara. Tokugawa Ieyasu's eastern army faced a western coalition led by Ishida Mitsunari in the heir's name. Western lord Kobayakawa Hideaki and others switched sides during the battle, and the coalition collapsed.",
 why="Toyotomi Hideyoshi's death in 1598 left a child heir, and the regents he appointed fought for power.",
 then="Tokugawa Ieyasu became shogun in 1603, confiscated or cut the lands of the losers and placed trusted allies in strategic domains. Defeated 'outside' lords, notably Chōshū and Satsuma, nursed the grievance for two and a half centuries and led the overthrow of the shogunate in 1868.",
 fact="Western lord Kobayakawa Hideaki's 15,000 men decided the battle by attacking their own side; Ieyasu is said to have fired on his position to force his hand."),
"as160316": dict(
 bluf="Tokugawa Ieyasu, shogun from 1603, set out to make his victory at Sekigahara permanent after a century of civil war. His house ruled for 250 years by freezing the political and social order: hereditary classes, restricted foreign contact and forced attendance of the lords at Edo. The peace produced an urban, literate, commercial society that modernised fast after 1868, and a regime that could not answer Western warships when they came in 1853.",
 now="Two and a half centuries of enforced peace gave Japan a unified market, a capital of a million people and high literacy by 1850, the base on which the Meiji state industrialised; the same controls left the shogunate unable to respond when Western warships arrived in 1853.",
 story="In 1603 Emperor Go-Yōzei named Tokugawa Ieyasu shogun, and Ieyasu ruled from his castle town of Edo, now Tokyo. His heirs required the great lords to spend alternate years at Edo and to leave their families behind as hostages, which kept the lords loyal and drained their wealth. Japan's population, cities and commerce grew through the 1600s.",
 why="Victory at Sekigahara left Tokugawa Ieyasu the strongest lord in Japan, and a century of civil war made peace and order the overriding goal.",
 then="Edo became one of the largest cities in the world and literacy spread. When Western warships arrived in 1853 the system could not respond, and the shogunate fell in 1868.",
 fact="By 1700 Edo was probably the largest city in the world, with about a million people."),
"as163918": dict(
 bluf="Japanese shogun Tokugawa Iemitsu wanted Christianity and foreign influence out of Japan and foreign trade under the shogunate's own control. In 1639 he expelled the Portuguese, completing a decade of edicts that banned the faith and forbade Japanese to leave; only the Dutch kept a post at Nagasaki. The regime was secured against foreign-backed revolt for two centuries, and Japan met the industrial West in 1853 with no navy and no modern army.",
 now="The restrictions explain why Japan's encounter with Western power in 1853 was so sudden and why its modernisation after 1868 was so rapid and state-driven: the state that had controlled contact could redirect it.",
 story="Christian missions had won perhaps 300,000 converts in Japan by the early 1600s. Japanese shogun Tokugawa Iemitsu's government banned Christianity, forbade Japanese from leaving the country in 1635 and expelled the Portuguese traders in 1639. Only the Dutch could trade, confined to a small island in Nagasaki harbour.",
 why="The shogunate saw Christianity, with perhaps 300,000 converts, as a threat to its authority, and wanted to control foreign trade itself.",
 trig="The Shimabara Rebellion of 1637-38, a peasant revolt with many Christian participants, convinced the shogunate that Portuguese missionaries were dangerous.",
 then="Trade continued with China, Korea, the Ryukyu kingdom and the Dutch, so Japan stayed partly open, and Dutch books fed a body of 'Dutch learning'. Japan developed on its own terms until US warships forced it open in 1853.",
 fact="Dutch traders were confined to Dejima, a small artificial fan-shaped island in Nagasaki harbour."),
"as164419": dict(
 bluf="Rebel leader Li Zicheng marched on Beijing in 1644 to replace a Ming dynasty that could no longer pay its soldiers or feed its people. His army entered the capital in April and the Chongzhen Emperor hanged himself. A dynasty that had ruled since 1368 ended, and the Manchus took the capital from Li within six weeks and founded the Qing, China's last imperial dynasty.",
 now="The Ming collapse is the standard case of fiscal crisis, climate shock and rebellion toppling a state together; it gave China a Manchu dynasty whose expanded borders the People's Republic still claims.",
 story="Decades of famine, epidemic, unpaid soldiers and an empty treasury fed peasant rebellions across north China. Rebel leader Li Zicheng's army entered Beijing in April 1644. The Chongzhen Emperor hanged himself on a hill behind the Forbidden City. The Ming had ruled since 1368.",
 why="The 'Little Ice Age' brought decades of cold, drought and famine. Disruption to the silver supply the tax system depended on and military costs on the northern frontier emptied the treasury. Factional paralysis at court left the government unable to respond, and unpaid soldiers and starving peasants joined the rebel armies.",
 then="Li Zicheng held Beijing for six weeks. Ming general Wu Sangui let the Manchus through the Great Wall at Shanhai Pass, and they took the capital in June 1644 and proclaimed the Qing.",
 fact="The last Ming emperor hanged himself on Coal Hill, Jingshan, behind the Forbidden City as the rebels took Beijing."),
"as164419b": dict(
 bluf="Manchu regent Dorgon wanted the Ming throne for his child emperor, and Ming general Wu Sangui wanted the rebels who held Beijing destroyed. Wu opened Shanhai Pass, the two armies routed Li Zicheng's rebels and the Manchus took Beijing in June 1644. The Qing ruled China until 1912 and roughly doubled its territory, adding Tibet, Xinjiang, Mongolia and Taiwan; the borders of today's People's Republic largely follow that expansion.",
 now="The People's Republic grounds its claims to Tibet, Xinjiang and Taiwan in the Qing borders, while Taiwan's government and Tibetan and Uyghur exile bodies contest what those borders meant; the Qing decline after 1839 begins the 'century of humiliation' central to the Communist Party's story of itself.",
 story="Rebel leader Li Zicheng took Beijing in April 1644. Ming general Wu Sangui, holding Shanhai Pass where the Great Wall meets the sea, let the Manchu army under regent Dorgon through. Together they routed the rebels, and the Manchus occupied Beijing in June 1644 and proclaimed the Qing dynasty rulers of all China. Conquering the south took nearly forty more years.",
 why="The Manchus, a state built north of the Wall, saw the Ming collapse as their chance. Ming general Wu Sangui wanted revenge on the rebels.",
 then="Qing emperors ruled through Chinese institutions while keeping a separate Manchu identity, and in 1645 ordered Chinese men to shave the front of the head and wear the Manchu queue on pain of death. The long reigns of the Kangxi, Yongzheng and Qianlong emperors in the 1700s brought prosperity and expansion. The dynasty struggled to respond to Western powers after 1839.",
 fact="The Qing Shunzhi Emperor was six when he was enthroned in Beijing; his uncle Dorgon held the real power until his death in 1650."),
"as166120": dict(
 bluf="The Qing Kangxi Emperor, enthroned at seven in 1661, set out to make Manchu rule over China secure and accepted. Over 61 years he crushed the last internal rebellion, took Taiwan, fixed the Russian border and won over much of the Chinese official class through Confucian patronage. The Qing became a stable, expanding empire, and the pattern of a Manchu dynasty governing through Chinese institutions held until 1911.",
 now="Kangxi's sixty-one years fixed Qing rule over China, Taiwan, Mongolia and the Russian frontier, and set the pattern of a Manchu dynasty governing through Confucian institutions that held until 1911.",
 story="The Qing Shunzhi Emperor died of smallpox in 1661 and his seven-year-old son became the Kangxi Emperor under a regency. At 15 Kangxi took personal power by arresting his overbearing regent, Oboi. Over a 61-year reign he crushed the Revolt of the Three Feudatories, took Taiwan in 1683 and fixed the border with Russia in 1689. He studied mathematics and astronomy with Jesuit advisers such as Ferdinand Verbiest.",
 why="The Shunzhi Emperor died of smallpox, and Kangxi was chosen partly because he had already survived the disease. A Manchu minority ruling China needed Chinese officials invested in the dynasty.",
 then="The Kangxi Emperor patronised Confucian learning, which kept Chinese elites invested in the dynasty, and tolerated the Jesuits until a dispute with Rome over Chinese rites. Under his grandson the Qianlong Emperor the empire reached its greatest extent.",
 fact="His grandson the Qianlong Emperor abdicated in 1796 after 60 years on the throne so as not to reign longer than Kangxi."),
"as168321": dict(
 bluf="The Qing Kangxi Emperor sent a fleet to destroy the Zheng family's Ming-loyalist base on Taiwan and secure the China coast. Admiral Shi Lang won at Penghu in 1683 and Koxinga's grandson surrendered. The island came under Chinese imperial rule for the first time, for two centuries, and that period is the historical basis on which Beijing claims Taiwan today and which Taipei reads differently.",
 now="Whether Taiwan is part of China is among the most dangerous disputes in world politics, and both sides draw on this history: Beijing cites two centuries of Qing rule as proof the island is Chinese; Taipei notes that the Qing ceded it in 1895 and that no government in Beijing has ruled it since.",
 story="After the Ming fell, Ming loyalist general Koxinga drove the Dutch out of Taiwan in 1662, and his family ruled the island as a base against the Qing. In 1683 a Qing fleet under Admiral Shi Lang won the Battle of Penghu. Koxinga's grandson Zheng Keshuang surrendered.",
 why="The Zheng family used Taiwan as a base for Ming loyalist resistance and maritime trade, and the Qing needed to eliminate it to secure the coast.",
 then="The Qing at first ruled Taiwan reluctantly as part of Fujian province and restricted migration; Han Chinese settlers came anyway and displaced Indigenous peoples. Taiwan became a province in 1885 and was ceded to Japan in 1895.",
 fact="The Qing court debated abandoning the island after conquering it; Admiral Shi Lang argued it would become a pirate base and won."),
"d-nerchinsk": dict(
 bluf="The Qing Kangxi Emperor wanted Russia out of the Amur valley and away from the Mongols; Russia wanted trade with China more than forts on the Amur. At Nerchinsk in 1689 the two agreed a border on the Argun River and the Stanovoy mountains, with Russia giving up the Amur. China's first treaty with a European state was made on equal terms, and the border held for 170 years.",
 now="The contrast between Nerchinsk, made on equal terms, and the 'unequal treaties' after 1842 remains part of Chinese historical memory, and the Amur line it drew is the one Russia tore up in 1858-60.",
 story="Russian forts on the Amur River had clashed with Qing forces through the 1680s. Qing envoy Songgotu and Russian envoy Fyodor Golovin met at Nerchinsk in Siberia in 1689. They agreed a border along the Argun River and the Stanovoy mountains, and Russia gave up the Amur valley. Jesuits in Qing service, Jean-François Gerbillon and Thomas Pereira, translated, so the official text was in Latin.",
 why="The Qing wanted to secure Manchuria and keep Russia away from the Mongols. Russia wanted trade.",
 then="The border held until Russia, taking advantage of Qing weakness, annexed the Amur region in 1858 and the Pacific coast in 1860.",
 fact="The treaty's official text was in Latin, the only language the two sides' interpreters shared."),
"jp-rangaku": dict(
 bluf="Japanese shogun Tokugawa Yoshimune wanted better calendars, astronomy and farming, and in 1720 he lifted the ban on Western books other than Christian ones. Japanese doctors and astronomers began studying European science through Dutch texts landed at Nagasaki. Over the next century 'Dutch learning' built a body of scientific knowledge and trained translators, the base that made modernisation after 1853 fast.",
 now="It explains why Japan could modernise so fast after 1868: a century of translators, doctors and gunnery students trained on Dutch texts was already in place.",
 story="The shogunate had banned Western books along with Christianity. Japanese shogun Tokugawa Yoshimune wanted better calendars and farming, and in 1720 he allowed in Western books that contained no Christian teaching. Japanese students began on medicine, astronomy and geography through Dutch texts brought to Nagasaki.",
 why="The shogunate needed practical knowledge, especially for astronomy and the calendar.",
 then="Students of Dutch learning translated anatomy, chemistry and gunnery books over the next century, beginning with an anatomy text in 1774.",
 fact="The field was called 'rangaku', from 'Oranda', the Japanese name for Holland, and 'gaku', learning."),
"jp-kaitai": dict(
 bluf="Physicians Sugita Genpaku and Maeno Ryōtaku set out to test a Dutch anatomy book against a dissected body, then to translate it. The book proved accurate where Chinese models failed, and after three years with almost no dictionary they published 'Kaitai Shinsho' in 1774. 'Dutch learning' became a scientific movement rather than a curiosity, and Japan had Western-trained doctors and translators eighty years before Perry arrived.",
 now="It shows Japan's habit of selective borrowing: learning foreign knowledge to strengthen itself while deciding for itself what to adopt.",
 story="In 1771 physician Sugita Genpaku watched the dissection of an executed criminal at Edo with a Dutch anatomy book in hand and found the book's plates accurate. He, physician Maeno Ryōtaku and colleagues spent three years translating it with almost no dictionary. 'Kaitai Shinsho', the New Book of Anatomy, appeared in 1774.",
 why="Japanese doctors trained in Chinese medicine wanted to test it against Western anatomy, and the shogunate's partial lifting of the book ban in 1720 had made Dutch texts available.",
 then="Dutch learning spread from medicine to astronomy, chemistry and gunnery, giving Japan a body of Western-trained students before Perry arrived in 1853.",
 fact="Physician Sugita Genpaku recalled that the team began knowing scarcely more Dutch than the alphabet, and spent whole days on single words."),
"as179325": dict(
 bluf="British envoy Lord Macartney went to Beijing in 1793 to win open ports, fixed tariffs and a resident embassy. The Qianlong Emperor received him and refused every request, writing to King George III that China possessed all things and needed no British manufactures. Britain's tea deficit kept growing; it turned to opium and then to force, and the Opium War of 1839 opened China on Britain's terms.",
 now="It is often taught as China's missed chance to join the industrial age. One reading holds that Qianlong's refusal reflected ignorance of the outside world; the other holds that the Qing understood Britain well enough and judged that open trade and a resident embassy threatened a system that was working for them.",
 story="British envoy Lord Macartney sailed to China with about 700 people and gifts including telescopes, clocks and a planetarium, hoping to open more ports, set up a permanent embassy and fix tariffs. He refused to perform the full kowtow and knelt on one knee instead. The Qianlong Emperor received him politely and rejected every request, writing to George III that China possessed all things and had no need of British manufactures.",
 why="China saw itself as the centre of civilisation, receiving foreigners as tribute-bearers. Britain wanted diplomatic equality, more open ports and a resident ambassador.",
 then="Britain's trade deficit from buying tea kept growing, and it turned to selling Indian opium to China, which led to the Opium Wars after 1839.",
 fact="The only member of the mission who could speak Chinese was a 12-year-old page, George Thomas Staunton, who had learned it on the voyage; the emperor gave him a purse from his own belt."),
"jp-phaeton": dict(
 bluf="The British frigate HMS Phaeton entered Nagasaki in October 1808 to seize Dutch ships, since Napoleon controlled the Netherlands. It took Dutch hostages, demanded supplies and left when they were delivered; Japan's coastal defences could do nothing. Nagasaki magistrate Matsudaira Yasuhide killed himself over the failure, and shogunate policy hardened into the 1825 order to fire on foreign ships.",
 now="It showed early that isolation could not stop a modern navy, a lesson Japan fully absorbed only after Perry arrived in 1853.",
 story="Britain was at war with Napoleon, who controlled the Netherlands, so British warships hunted Dutch shipping. HMS Phaeton under Captain Fleetwood Pellew entered Nagasaki harbour in October 1808 flying a Dutch flag, seized two Dutch hostages and demanded food and water. The Japanese garrison was far below strength and could not respond. The ship left after two days with its supplies.",
 why="Britain was at war with Napoleon, who controlled the Netherlands, so British warships hunted Dutch ships even at Nagasaki, the only port open to the Dutch.",
 then="Nagasaki magistrate Matsudaira Yasuhide took his own life for the failure. Japan grew more fearful of Western warships, and the shogunate ordered them fired on in 1825.",
 fact="Phaeton flew a Dutch flag to get into the harbour; the Dutch officials who rowed out to greet it became its hostages."),
"jp-repel": dict(
 bluf="The Tokugawa shogunate ordered coastal guards in 1825 to fire on any foreign ship without warning, to keep Western whalers and warships, and Christianity with them, away from Japan. Guards obeyed when they could, firing on a US merchant ship in 1837. The order failed to deter the ships and was withdrawn in 1842 after China's defeat by Britain; isolation could no longer be enforced.",
 now="It shows how isolation was becoming impossible to enforce; within two decades the policy had been abandoned.",
 story="Western whalers and warships appeared off Japan with growing frequency after 1800. In 1825 the shogunate under Japanese shogun Tokugawa Ienari issued the Edict to Repel Foreign Ships. Guards were to fire without warning, even at ships carrying shipwrecked sailors home.",
 why="More Western whalers and warships were appearing off Japan, and the shogunate feared that Christianity and foreign influence would spread.",
 then="In 1837 coastal guns fired on the US merchant ship Morrison, which was trying to return Japanese castaways. The shogunate replaced the order in 1842 with one allowing foreign ships food, water and fuel.",
 fact="The edict was known as the 'fire without a second thought' order, after the instruction that guards should open fire on sight."),
"as183927": dict(
 bluf="Britain went to war in 1839 to protect the opium trade that paid for its tea and to force China open to Western commerce on Western terms. Its warships outgunned Qing forces along the coast and up the Yangtze. China lost the ability to set the terms of its foreign relations, and the 'century of humiliation' that Chinese nationalism still invokes began with the Treaty of Nanjing in 1842.",
 now="China calls this the start of its 'century of humiliation', a theme that frames its politics and its foreign policy today.",
 story="The East India Company grew opium in India and smuggled it into China to pay for tea. By the 1830s millions of Chinese were addicted and silver was flowing out. In 1839 Qing Commissioner Lin Zexu seized and destroyed about 20,000 chests of opium at Canton. British Foreign Secretary Lord Palmerston sent warships, which outgunned Chinese forces along the coast and up the Yangtze.",
 why="Britain bought large quantities of tea while China wanted few British goods, and Britain closed the gap with opium. China tried to suppress the drug trade. Britain also demanded diplomatic equality and open trade.",
 trig="Commissioner Lin Zexu's destruction of British opium at Canton in 1839.",
 then="The Treaty of Nanjing in 1842 ceded Hong Kong, opened five ports and imposed an indemnity; later treaties exempted foreigners from Chinese law. Opium imports kept growing, and a Second Opium War in 1856-60 forced further concessions.",
 fact="Commissioner Lin Zexu wrote to Queen Victoria asking how Britain would feel if China sold poison to its people. The letter probably never reached her."),
"as184229": dict(
 bluf="Britain wanted its opium losses paid, Hong Kong as a base and China's ports open; the Qing wanted British warships off the Yangtze. The treaty signed aboard HMS Cornwallis in August 1842 gave Britain 21 million silver dollars, five ports and Hong Kong Island in perpetuity. It was the first of the 'unequal treaties', and its model of ports, indemnities and privileges set the pattern of Western dominance in China for a century.",
 now="Hong Kong's return in 1997 and the 'century of humiliation' narrative make Nanjing a lasting symbol in Chinese politics.",
 story="British warships had reached the Yangtze and threatened Nanjing in the summer of 1842. The treaty was signed aboard the warship HMS Cornwallis at Nanjing on 29 August 1842. China agreed to pay 21 million silver dollars, open five ports to British trade and residence and cede Hong Kong Island 'in perpetuity'.",
 why="British warships had cut the Grand Canal and threatened Nanjing, which forced the Qing to make peace.",
 then="France and the United States demanded similar treaties in 1844, and 'most-favoured nation' clauses meant any concession to one power went to all.",
 fact="Hong Kong was then a barely inhabited rock; British Foreign Secretary Lord Palmerston called it a barren island with hardly a house on it."),
"jp-1842": dict(
 bluf="Senior councillor Mizuno Tadakuni wanted to spare Japan the fate of China, which a few British warships had just beaten in the Opium War. In 1842 the shogunate replaced its order to fire on foreign ships with one allowing them food, water and fuel, and began studying Western gunnery. The reversal shows the pragmatism that later drove Japan's modernisation, and it came from a neighbour's defeat rather than Japan's own.",
 now="It shows that a neighbour's defeat can change policy faster than one's own experience: China's 'century of humiliation' was Japan's warning.",
 story="News of China's defeat by Britain in the Opium War reached Japan through Dutch and Chinese traders at Nagasaki. Senior councillor Mizuno Tadakuni and his colleagues replaced the 1825 firing order with one allowing foreign ships to receive food, water and fuel.",
 why="News reached Japan that China, the giant of Asia, had been humbled by a few British warships, and officials feared Japan would be next.",
 then="Japanese reformers began studying Western gunnery and coastal defence. The shogunate still refused to open the country, and held that line until Perry's arrival in 1853.",
 fact="The Dutch at Dejima supplied the shogunate each year with a written summary of world news, the 'fūsetsugaki', which is how word of the Opium War arrived."),
"as185030": dict(
 bluf="Hong Xiuquan, a failed examination candidate who believed himself the younger brother of Jesus, set out to overthrow the Qing and build a Heavenly Kingdom of equality. His armies took Nanjing in 1853 and held the Yangtze valley for eleven years before Qing forces retook it in 1864. The war killed perhaps 20 to 30 million people, and the Qing survived only by relying on regional armies and Western help, which permanently weakened central authority.",
 now="The regional armies the Qing raised to survive it became the power bases of warlordism after 1911; both Nationalists and Communists later claimed the Taiping as revolutionary forerunners.",
 story="Hong Xiuquan failed the civil service examinations, read Christian tracts and came to believe he was the younger brother of Jesus. His movement grew among the poor and the Hakka minority of southern China. In 1853 the Taiping took Nanjing, made it their 'Heavenly Capital' and ruled much of the Yangtze valley. Qing forces retook the city in 1864.",
 why="Population pressure and poverty in the south came first. Qing weakness after the Opium War and ethnic tension between the Hakka and their neighbours gave the movement its recruits. A charismatic religious message promising equality held it together.",
 then="The Qing relied on regional armies raised by Chinese officials such as Zeng Guofan and Li Hongzhang, and on Western-officered units; these regional power bases later fed warlordism. The devastation of the Yangtze valley set back China's economy for decades.",
 fact="Its death toll was comparable to the whole of the First World War, yet it is barely taught in the West."),
"as185331": dict(
 bluf="US Commodore Matthew Perry sailed into Edo Bay in July 1853 to force Japan to open ports to American ships, under orders to use the threat of his guns. The shogunate, with no navy able to resist, accepted his letter and signed a treaty when he returned in 1854. Two centuries of restricted contact ended, the shogunate's authority was discredited, and the crisis that followed produced the Meiji Restoration of 1868.",
 now="Japan's transformation into a modern power begins here, and the 'black ships' remain the standard example of an outside shock forcing a closed society to change.",
 story="In July 1853 US Commodore Matthew Perry sailed four ships, two of them steam-powered, into Edo Bay and delivered a letter from President Millard Fillmore demanding trade and good treatment for shipwrecked sailors. He promised to return the next year with a larger fleet. Japan had no navy able to resist.",
 why="The United States wanted coaling stations for steamships on the route to China, protection for its whalers and new markets. China's defeat by Britain had shown how vulnerable Asian states were.",
 then="The Treaty of Kanagawa in 1854 and the unequal treaties of 1858 followed. Anger at the shogunate's surrender fed a movement to restore the emperor, and the shogunate fell in 1868.",
 fact="Among Perry's gifts was a working miniature steam train that Japanese officials rode around a track."),
"jp-kanagawa": dict(
 bluf="US Commodore Matthew Perry returned to Japan in 1854 with nine ships to get the treaty he had demanded the year before. The shogunate agreed on 31 March to open Shimoda and Hakodate, help shipwrecked sailors and accept a US consul. Japan's first treaty with a Western power ended over 200 years of restricted contact and set off the internal crisis that toppled the shogunate within 15 years.",
 now="It ended over 200 years of isolation and set off the crisis that brought down the shogunate within 15 years, the starting point of Japan's modernisation.",
 story="US Commodore Matthew Perry came back in February 1854 with nine ships. The shogunate agreed to open two small ports, Shimoda and Hakodate, to help shipwrecked sailors and to accept a US consul. The treaty was signed at Kanagawa, now part of Yokohama, on 31 March 1854.",
 why="Japan had no navy that could resist, and its leaders wanted to avoid China's fate in the Opium War.",
 then="US consul Townsend Harris arrived in 1856 and pressed for full trade, which led to the unequal treaties of 1858.",
 fact="US consul Townsend Harris opened the first American consulate in Japan in a Buddhist temple at Shimoda."),
"jp-harris": dict(
 bluf="US consul Townsend Harris pressed Japan for a full commercial treaty, warning that Britain and France would impose worse terms by force. Chief minister Ii Naosuke signed in July 1858 without the emperor's approval, and Britain, France, Russia and the Netherlands took the same terms. The treaties gave Western powers extraterritorial rights and control of Japan's tariffs; outrage turned against the shogunate, and revising them became the central goal of Meiji Japan.",
 now="Undoing these treaties became the central goal of Meiji foreign policy, achieved only in 1899 for extraterritoriality and 1911 for tariffs.",
 story="Chief minister Ii Naosuke signed the Harris Treaty aboard the USS Powhatan in Edo Bay on 29 July 1858 after Emperor Kōmei refused to approve it. Britain, France, Russia and the Netherlands quickly got the same terms: more open ports, extraterritoriality, with foreigners tried in their own courts, and low fixed tariffs.",
 why="US consul Townsend Harris warned that Britain and France, fresh from beating China again, would impose worse terms by force.",
 then="Opponents rallied around the emperor, and chief minister Ii Naosuke launched the Ansei Purge against them in 1858-59. He was assassinated in 1860.",
 fact="US consul Townsend Harris waited two years at Shimoda before being received in Edo; his audience with the shogun in December 1857 was the first granted to a foreign diplomat."),
"as186034": dict(
 bluf="British and French commanders in the Second Opium War wanted the Qing court punished for the torture and killing of their envoys and forced to accept their terms. In October 1860 British envoy Lord Elgin had the Yuanmingyuan palace gardens looted and burned. The Qing signed the Convention of Peking within days, legalising opium and opening Beijing and the interior, and the burning became China's defining symbol of Western humiliation.",
 now="The burning of the palace at the end of the Second Opium War forced the Qing to open Beijing to foreign legations and the interior to trade and missionaries; it is the act China's government cites as the start of its century of humiliation.",
 story="British and French troops reached Beijing in October 1860 during the Second Opium War, and the Xianfeng Emperor fled to Jehol. Qing officials had tortured and killed members of an Allied negotiating party. British envoy Lord Elgin ordered the Yuanmingyuan palace gardens, the Summer Palace, looted and burned.",
 why="Revenge for the torture and killing of envoys, and pressure on the emperor to accept Allied terms.",
 then="China legalised opium, opened more ports and ceded Kowloon to Britain. Russia used the moment to take China's Pacific coast north of Korea in the Treaty of Peking of 1860.",
 fact="The ruins are a national memorial, and China asks for the looted treasures back."),
"jp-sakurada": dict(
 bluf="Samurai from Mito domain set out to kill chief minister Ii Naosuke for signing the unequal treaties and purging their allies. Eighteen of them ambushed his procession in the snow outside Edo Castle's Sakurada Gate in March 1860 and took his head. The shogunate had shown it could neither protect its own leaders nor control samurai anger, and a cycle of political violence began that ended in the regime's fall in 1868.",
 now="It is a turning point: the regime's authority visibly cracked, and assassination became a recurring feature of Japanese politics into the 1930s.",
 story="Chief minister Ii Naosuke had signed the unequal treaties of 1858 and purged his critics in the Ansei Purge. On a snowy morning in March 1860, 18 samurai, 17 from Mito and one from Satsuma, ambushed his procession at the Sakurada Gate of Edo Castle and cut off his head.",
 why="Rage at the treaties and the purge, and a belief that the shogun had betrayed the emperor.",
 then="Attacks on foreigners and officials multiplied, and the shogunate's authority visibly crumbled.",
 fact="The shogunate concealed Ii's death for weeks, reporting him wounded, while it arranged the succession to his domain."),
"jp-kagoshima": dict(
 bluf="Britain sent a squadron to Kagoshima in August 1863 to force Satsuma domain to pay for the killing of merchant Charles Richardson and hand over his killers. The ships burned much of the town and lost 13 men; Satsuma paid and then bought British warships. One of Japan's most anti-foreign domains concluded it had to learn from the West rather than fight it, and Satsuma and Chōshū went on to lead Meiji modernisation.",
 now="Defeat taught Japan's most anti-foreign domains that they had to modernise rather than 'expel the barbarians'.",
 story="In September 1862 Satsuma samurai killed British merchant Charles Richardson, who had failed to give way to the procession of Satsuma regent Shimazu Hisamitsu, in what became known as the Namamugi Incident. Britain demanded compensation and the killers. When Satsuma refused, a Royal Navy squadron bombarded Kagoshima in August 1863 and burned much of the town.",
 why="Britain demanded punishment for Richardson's killing.",
 then="Satsuma paid the indemnity, bought warships from Britain and sent students to Britain in 1865. Chōshū learned the same lesson when Western fleets bombarded Shimonoseki in 1863-64.",
 fact="Satsuma's shore guns killed 13 British sailors, among them the captain of the flagship."),
"jp-satcho": dict(
 bluf="Satsuma and Chōshū, long enemies, wanted modern weapons and a government able to stand up to the West; Tosa go-between Sakamoto Ryōma brokered a secret alliance between them in Kyoto in 1866. Satsuma would supply Chōshū with Western arms and back it against the shogun. Japan's two most powerful anti-shogunate domains now acted together, which made the overthrow of the shogunate possible, and their leaders ran the Meiji government.",
 now="The alliance built the coalition that overthrew the shogunate in 1868 and supplied the men who ran the Meiji government; Sakamoto Ryōma, who brokered it, remains a national model of pragmatic coalition-building.",
 story="Satsuma and Chōshū had fought each other in Kyoto in 1864. Sakamoto Ryōma, a former Tosa samurai, brokered a secret alliance in Kyoto in 1866 between Satsuma leader Saigō Takamori and Chōshū leader Kido Takayoshi. Satsuma would help Chōshū buy Western weapons and would resist the shogun's attacks on it.",
 why="Both had learned they needed modern weapons and a new government that could stand up to the West.",
 then="Chōshū defeated the shogun's punitive expedition in 1866, which showed the shogunate's weakness.",
 fact="Go-between Sakamoto Ryōma was assassinated in Kyoto in December 1867 at 31, a year before the restoration he had worked for."),
"jp-taisei": dict(
 bluf="Japanese shogun Tokugawa Yoshinobu returned governing authority to the emperor in November 1867 to avoid civil war and keep his house at the head of a new council of lords. The surrender ended nearly 700 years of rule by military shoguns. His rivals in Satsuma and Chōshū moved within weeks to shut the Tokugawa out entirely, and the coup of January 1868 followed.",
 now="It ended nearly 700 years of rule by military shoguns and returned formal power to the emperor, the foundation on which the modern Japanese state was built.",
 story="Under pressure from the Satsuma-Chōshū alliance, Japanese shogun Tokugawa Yoshinobu announced at Nijō Castle in Kyoto in November 1867 that he was returning governing authority to the 15-year-old Emperor Meiji. He expected to lead a council of lords under the throne.",
 why="Shogun Tokugawa Yoshinobu hoped to avoid civil war and keep the Tokugawa family on top.",
 then="The Satsuma-Chōshū coup of 3 January 1868 proclaimed 'imperial restoration' and stripped the Tokugawa of lands and office.",
 fact="Tokugawa Yoshinobu lived until 1913, longer than any other shogun, and was made a duke by the government that had deposed him."),
"as186835": dict(
 bluf="Reformist samurai from Satsuma and Chōshū seized the Kyoto palace in January 1868 to replace the shogunate with rule in the emperor's name and build a state that could resist the West. Within a generation their government had abolished the domains and the samurai, built railways, factories, schools and a conscript army, and adopted a constitution. Japan became the first non-Western industrial and imperial power; the same structure concentrated power in an oligarchy and a military answerable only to the emperor.",
 now="Japan became the first non-Western industrial nation, and its state-led development became a model for East Asia's later 'tiger' economies.",
 story="In January 1868 reformist samurai from Satsuma and Chōshū seized the palace in Kyoto and proclaimed the restoration of direct rule by the 15-year-old Emperor Meiji. Over the following decades the new government abolished the feudal domains and the samurai class, built railways, factories and a conscript army, and introduced universal schooling. In 1889 it adopted a constitution modelled on Prussia's.",
 why="Fear of being colonised like China came first. The shogunate's humiliation by the unequal treaties discredited it. Ambitious samurai from the outer domains had learned that Western technology was the key to power.",
 then="Japan revised the unequal treaties by 1911, defeated China in 1895 and Russia in 1905, and annexed Taiwan and Korea. Modernisation concentrated power in a small oligarchy and a military answerable only to the emperor, a structure militarists later exploited.",
 fact="In about 40 years Japan went from samurai with swords to a navy that sank Russia's fleet."),
"jp-boshin": dict(
 bluf="The new imperial government fought to destroy the Tokugawa as a power, and Tokugawa loyalists fought to keep a place in the new order. Imperial troops won at Toba-Fushimi in January 1868, took Edo without a fight and crushed the northern domains and a last stand in Hokkaido in 1869. The war was short and limited, which let the Meiji state consolidate quickly and abolish the samurai order within a decade.",
 now="The war destroyed the shogunate and the domains that fought for it, and put the Meiji government in sole control of Japan, which let it abolish the samurai order within a decade.",
 story="Tokugawa forces clashed with imperial troops from Satsuma and Chōshū at Toba-Fushimi near Kyoto in January 1868 and lost. Edo surrendered peacefully in May after talks between Satsuma leader Saigō Takamori and Tokugawa retainer Katsu Kaishū. Northern domains led by Aizu fought on and fell that autumn. Loyalists under naval commander Enomoto Takeaki made a last stand at Hakodate in Hokkaido in 1869.",
 why="The Tokugawa refused to be shut out of the new government entirely, and northern domains feared the dominance of Satsuma and Chōshū.",
 then="The capital moved to Edo, renamed Tokyo, 'eastern capital', in 1868. The new government abolished the domains in 1871.",
 fact="Aizu's defeat is still remembered in Fukushima, where the losers keep their own version of the war."),
"jp-charter": dict(
 bluf="The new Meiji government, installed by a coup and still fighting the Tokugawa, needed to win over the domains and show it would rule by consensus and reform. In April 1868 Emperor Meiji swore five principles: deliberative assemblies, unity of all classes, freedom to pursue careers, an end to 'evil customs of the past' and the search for knowledge worldwide. The oath gave sweeping change the emperor's authority, and the government used it to dismantle the feudal order within three years.",
 now="The oath put the emperor's name on reform, which let the government dismantle the feudal order without a constitution; after the Second World War Emperor Hirohito cited it to argue that democracy had Japanese roots.",
 story="On 6 April 1868 Emperor Meiji swore an oath before the court nobles and lords in Kyoto. It set out five principles: deliberative assemblies, unity of all classes, freedom to pursue careers, abandoning 'evil customs of the past' and seeking knowledge worldwide. Reformers Yuri Kimimasa and Fukuoka Takachika drafted it, and Chōshū leader Kido Takayoshi revised it.",
 why="The new regime, installed by a coup and still fighting the Tokugawa, needed to win over the domains and show that it would rule by consensus and reform.",
 then="The government began dismantling the feudal order: the domains were abolished in 1871, and class restrictions on jobs and marriage were lifted.",
 fact="Emperor Hirohito's New Year rescript of 1946, renouncing his divinity, opened by quoting the oath in full."),
"jp-iwakura": dict(
 bluf="Half of Japan's leadership, under court noble Iwakura Tomomi, sailed in December 1871 to renegotiate the unequal treaties and to study the states that had imposed them. The Western powers refused to renegotiate, so for 18 months the mission studied factories, schools, parliaments and armies. Its members came home convinced that domestic modernisation must come before foreign adventures, and blocked the plan to invade Korea in 1873.",
 now="It is a model of a country systematically studying others to modernise, an approach later echoed by South Korea, Singapore and China.",
 story="Court noble Iwakura Tomomi led about 100 officials and students, among them Ōkubo Toshimichi, Kido Takayoshi and Itō Hirobumi, from Yokohama in December 1871. The mission hoped to renegotiate the unequal treaties. Western governments refused, so the delegates studied factories, schools, parliaments and armies in the United States, Britain, Germany and elsewhere. It returned in 1873.",
 why="Japan's leaders wanted to renegotiate the unequal treaties and to understand the West well enough to catch up with it.",
 then="Returning members blocked a plan to invade Korea in 1873, which split the government and sent Saigō Takamori into opposition.",
 fact="The mission took five girls to be educated in America; the youngest, Tsuda Umeko, was six and later founded a women's college in Tokyo."),
"jp-prefectures": dict(
 bluf="The Meiji leaders from Satsuma, Chōshū and Tosa wanted central control of taxes, armies and law, and many domains were too deep in debt to resist. In August 1871 a single decree abolished the domains, pensioned the lords off to Tokyo and put governors appointed from the capital in their place, with the three domains' troops behind it. Rule by hereditary lords ended in a day, and Japan became a centralised state on the prefecture design it still uses.",
 now="It is one of the fastest dismantlings of feudalism anywhere, and Japan's centralised, prefecture-based state still follows its design.",
 story="Lords had already returned their lands to the emperor in 1869 and stayed on as governors. In August 1871 the government, led by Ōkubo Toshimichi, Kido Takayoshi and Saigō Takamori, abolished the domains entirely. Lords were pensioned off to Tokyo, and governors appointed from the capital took over.",
 why="A modern state needed central control of taxes, armies and law, and many domains were deep in debt. Leaders from Satsuma, Chōshū and Tosa backed abolition with troops.",
 then="Conscription and a national land tax, both in 1873, and the end of samurai privileges followed, which concentrated power in Tokyo.",
 fact="Over 300 prefectures replaced the domains at first; they were merged to 75 by the end of 1871 and to today's 47 by 1888."),
"jp-railway": dict(
 bluf="Meiji leaders wanted a railway to unify the country, move goods and troops and show the West Japan's commitment to its technology. The first line, 29 km from Shimbashi in Tokyo to the port of Yokohama, opened in October 1872, built with a British loan under British engineer Edmund Morel. The state went on to build a national network that knit the economy together, then sold lines to firms close to the government.",
 now="Japan's rail network, from the shinkansen to dense commuter lines, is among the world's best, rooted in this early state commitment.",
 story="Japan's first railway opened on 14 October 1872, running 29 km, 18 miles, between Shimbashi in Tokyo and Yokohama, the main port for foreign trade. It was built with a British loan under British engineer Edmund Morel, and Emperor Meiji rode it on opening day. Some officials had opposed borrowing abroad, fearing that foreign debt would bring foreign control.",
 why="Railways were the symbol and engine of Western power, and Meiji leaders saw them as essential to unify the country and move goods and troops.",
 then="Railways, telegraphs and factories spread fast, built by the state and later sold to private firms close to the government.",
 fact="British engineer Edmund Morel chose a narrow gauge of 1,067 mm, which Japan's conventional lines still use; he died of tuberculosis in 1871, a year before the line opened."),
"jp-conscription": dict(
 bluf="Army reformer Yamagata Aritomo wanted a mass army of commoners loyal to the emperor in place of a hereditary warrior class. The Conscription Ordinance of January 1873 made military service universal for men, on the Prussian and French model. Samurai lost their stipends and their swords by 1876, and the Imperial Army the ordinance created became a pillar of the Meiji state and later the engine of Japanese militarism.",
 now="The Imperial Army it created dominated Japan until 1945, one reason post-war Japan's constitution renounced war.",
 story="Army reformer Yamagata Aritomo, drawing on Prussian and French models, introduced universal military service for men in January 1873. Samurai lost their stipends in 1876 and the right to wear swords the same year.",
 why="A modern army needed mass conscripts rather than a hereditary warrior class, and Yamagata Aritomo saw national service as a way to build loyalty to the emperor.",
 then="Angry samurai, and peasants who resented the 'blood tax', rose in revolts that culminated in the Satsuma Rebellion of 1877.",
 fact="The ordinance called conscription a 'blood tax', translating a French phrase; peasants took it literally, and rumours that conscripts' blood would be drawn fed the riots."),
"jp-seikanron": dict(
 bluf="Councillor Saigō Takamori wanted a war with Korea to give displaced samurai a purpose and to punish Korea's refusal to recognise the Meiji government; councillor Ōkubo Toshimichi and the Iwakura Mission returnees insisted Japan reform first. The reformers won in October 1873 and Saigō left the government. Korea stayed a target, and Japan forced it open by gunboat in 1876.",
 now="It shows that Korea was in Japan's sights from the start of the Meiji era; the debate was over timing rather than the goal.",
 story="Korea refused to recognise the new Meiji government. Councillor Saigō Takamori proposed to go to Korea as an envoy and provoke a war that would give unemployed samurai a purpose. Councillor Ōkubo Toshimichi and the leaders returning from the Iwakura Mission argued that Japan must reform first, and they prevailed in October 1873.",
 why="Korea refused to recognise the new Meiji government, and samurai who had lost their privileges were looking for a cause.",
 then="Saigō Takamori and his allies left the government, and Saigō later led the Satsuma Rebellion of 1877. Japan forced Korea open by gunboat diplomacy instead, in 1876.",
 fact="Saigō Takamori offered to go to Korea without an escort and get himself killed, to give Japan its pretext."),
"as187636": dict(
 bluf="Meiji Japan wanted influence in Korea, which China claimed as a tributary, and used the gunboat diplomacy the West had used on Japan. After a warship provoked a clash at Ganghwa Island in 1875, Japan forced Korea in February 1876 to open three ports and grant Japanese residents extraterritorial rights. Korea was opened on unequal terms, and Japan's contest with China and Russia for control of it ended in annexation in 1910.",
 now="It opened Korea on unequal terms, beginning the Japanese pressure that ended in annexation in 1910 and the grievances that still shape Korea-Japan relations.",
 story="In 1875 the Japanese warship Un'yō provoked a clash with Korean forts on Ganghwa Island. Early the next year Japan sent warships and forced Korea's Joseon court to sign the Treaty of Ganghwa, opening three ports and giving Japanese residents extraterritorial rights.",
 why="Meiji Japan copied Western gunboat diplomacy to win influence in Korea, which China claimed as a tributary.",
 then="Japan competed with China and Russia for control of Korea and fought both of them, in 1894-95 and 1904-05.",
 fact="The treaty's first article declared Korea an independent state, wording aimed at cutting China's claim to it."),
"jp-satsuma": dict(
 bluf="Satsuma samurai, stripped of stipends, swords and status, rose under Saigō Takamori in January 1877 to reverse the Meiji reforms by force. The government's conscript army, with modern rifles and artillery, wore them down over eight months, and Saigō died at Shiroyama in September. The samurai ended as a political force, and opposition moved into the movement for a constitution and a parliament.",
 now="The conscript army's victory settled that the Meiji state could enforce its reforms; Saigō remains a beloved tragic hero, and the rebellion's memory shapes Japanese ideas of loyalty and sacrifice.",
 story="Samurai in Satsuma, furious at losing their stipends and swords, rose under Saigō Takamori in January 1877. The government's conscript army, with modern rifles and artillery, besieged them for months. Saigō died at Shiroyama in September 1877.",
 why="Samurai resented reforms that took away their stipends, swords and status, and Saigō Takamori had left the government after losing the 1873 debate over Korea.",
 then="Opposition turned to politics, in a movement for a constitution and a parliament that won an imperial promise of a Diet in 1881.",
 fact="The government pardoned Saigō Takamori posthumously in 1889, and his statue stands in Tokyo's Ueno Park."),
"jp-constitution": dict(
 bluf="Statesman Itō Hirobumi wanted a constitution that would satisfy demands for a parliament and prove to the West that Japan was 'civilised' enough to end the unequal treaties, while keeping power with the emperor and his ministers. The constitution of February 1889, on the Prussian model, gave Japan a parliament and a strong emperor. Asia had its first constitutional state; a military answerable only to the emperor, outside cabinet control, let militarists take charge in the 1930s.",
 now="Its weakness, the army answering only to the emperor, helped militarists take over in the 1930s.",
 story="After studying constitutions in Europe, statesman Itō Hirobumi chose Prussia's model, with a strong emperor and a limited parliament. Emperor Meiji promulgated the constitution as a 'gift' to his people on 11 February 1889. The Diet first met in 1890.",
 why="To show the West Japan was 'civilised' enough to end the unequal treaties, and to answer demands for a parliament.",
 then="Western powers began agreeing to revise the unequal treaties, starting with Britain in 1894, and the elected Diet slowly gained influence through its power over budgets.",
 fact="The promulgation date, 11 February, was the legendary founding day of Japan by Emperor Jimmu, then a national holiday."),
}
